 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Sorting is $O(n \log n)$ on the CPU [S5, unofficial]. Rendering is $O(n)$ for instanced drawing.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for splat data (textures/buffers), order buffer, and view data. For 233k splats, re-sorting involves ~5.6 MB of texture re-upload [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Fill rate and overdraw are dominant factors. Anisotropic rendering reduces overdraw compared to isotropic max-axis discs [S5, unofficial]. CPU sorting is feasible up to ~300–500k splats; beyond that, LOD/pruning is needed [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, leverages standard graphics APIs, good quality with anisotropic projection.
-*   **Limitations:** CPU sorting bottleneck at high splat counts, isotropic approximation causes visual artifacts (haze), requires careful culling to avoid artifacts from behind-camera splats.
-*   **When to Use:** Real-time visualization of static or slowly changing 3DGS scenes on desktop/mobile/web where CPU sorting is acceptable.
-*   **When Not to Use:** Extremely high splat counts (>500k) without LOD, or when GPU-based sorting is required for performance.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Sorting is $O(n \log n)$ performed on the CPU [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires texture re-upload of about 16 B/splat (24 B with a third texture) per re-sort [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** At 233k splats, re-sorting involves about 5.6 MB of data transfer [S5, unofficial]. Fill rate and overdraw dominate performance on mid-range phones [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** The CPU sort is fine up to about 300–500k splats; beyond that, LOD/pruning is needed [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering on client-side hardware; reduces overdraw compared to isotropic billboards (anisotropic variant); handles transparency via alpha blending [S1, S3, S5, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** CPU sorting can become a bottleneck for very large scenes; isotropic rendering causes haze and over-brightness; multiple Splatv objects are not supported [S3, S5, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Real-time visualization of 3D Gaussian Splatting scenes in web browsers or mobile apps where neural network inference is not feasible [S1, S3, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** Scenes with more than 500k splats without LOD; when isotropic rendering quality is insufficient (use anisotropic variant) [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Gaussian Splat Rendering (Screen-Space Quad) is based on the 3D Gaussian Splatting technique introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It adapts the mathematical representation of 3D Gaussians into a rasterization-friendly format by projecting them into 2D screen-space quads. The method has been implemented in various contexts, including Unity (aras-p/UnityGaussianSplatting) [S2, unofficial], WebGL (gsplat.js) [S3, unofficial], and SceneView [S4, S5, unofficial]. The screen-space quad approach is a common implementation strategy for visualizing Gaussian splats in real-time renderers.
+Gaussian Splat Rendering (Screen-Space Quad) is a rendering technique identical in geometry generation to Circle Splat, used for Gaussian Splat visualization, projecting voxels as 2D quads [S1, unofficial]. It is used in `gsplat.js` for real-time WebGL rendering of Gaussian splats [S3, unofficial]. The method builds on the general projection and quad-drawing mechanism described in [3D Gaussian Splatting Rendering (CPU-Sorted, Instanced)](2058-3d-gaussian-splatting-rendering-cpu-sorted-instanced.md) [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
